## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The replacement is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` with task-based execution.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws on timeout even if work ignores the token
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **Work ignores the token** — cancellation is *cooperative*; if `work` never checks, it keeps running in the background | Pass `CancellationToken` into `work` and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints |
| **Fire-and-forget leak** — the sync version abandons the task if timeout fires | Accept that uncooperative work will finish eventually; for truly uncooperative code, isolate in a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill` |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using` — leaks timers otherwise |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish timeout from caller cancellation |

### What Was Removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|--------------------|-------------------|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed — no equivalent needed |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync` |

### Critical Behavioral Difference

With `Thread.Abort`, the work was *forcibly terminated* mid-execution. With cooperative cancellation, **the work must opt in**. If `work` is a tight CPU loop or a blocking synchronous call (e.g., `socket.Receive`), it won't stop until it reaches a cancellation checkpoint. For long-running delegates, refactor them to accept `CancellationToken`:

```csharp
// Caller
var result = await executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(async ct =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        await ProcessAsync(item, ct);
    }
    return ComputeResult();
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```
